NAME
       userdel - delete a user account

SYNOPSIS
       userdel [-r] [-f] LOGIN

DESCRIPTION
       userdel removes LOGIN from /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow and the group
       files. The user's private group is removed too when no other account
       uses it as its primary group.

       Without -r, the home directory and the mail spool stay behind, still
       owned by the old UID number. Such files show a number instead of a
       name in ls -l, and a new account that later gets the same UID would
       own them. Files elsewhere on the system are never removed by userdel;
       find them with find / -nouser (or find / -uid UID) and decide what to
       do with them.

       userdel refuses to delete a user who is logged in or runs processes,
       unless -f is given.

OPTIONS
       -r, --remove
              Also remove the home directory and the mail spool
              (/var/spool/mail/LOGIN).

       -f, --force
              Delete even while the user is logged in, and remove the home
              directory even if another user owns it. Use with care.

EXAMPLES
       Delete the user harry with his home directory:

           userdel -r harry

       Delete an account but keep its files, then look for what is left:

           userdel olduser
           find / -nouser 2>/dev/null

NOTES
       To keep a person from logging in without deleting anything, lock the
       account instead (usermod -L, or chage -E 0).

SEE ALSO
       useradd(8), usermod(8), groupdel(8), find(1)
